Production-level engineering considerations
For a three-year-experience interview, knowing how to call a tokenizer is only the beginning. You should also understand the decisions around it.
1. Token budget management
If your prompt contains 8,000 tokens and your output budget is 1,000 tokens, you need enough context capacity for both, plus any applicable request formatting and model-specific requirements.
2. Language differences
Tamil, English, code, and mixed-language text may produce different token counts. Don't assume that a fixed number of characters always equals a fixed number of tokens.
3. Cost and latency
Longer inputs and outputs can increase processing time and, for token-billed APIs, cost. Measure actual usage rather than relying entirely on character-based estimates.
4. Batch processing
When processing thousands of support tickets, consider batching tokenizer inputs where supported. Hugging Face fast tokenizers can be particularly useful for batch processing. 
5. Error handling
Your application should handle unavailable model repositories, missing credentials, API rate limits, network failures, and inputs that exceed supported limits.
6. Security
Never put customer account numbers, transaction records, API keys, or other sensitive production data into public code examples or unnecessary logs.
