Beginner level
Q1. What is tokenization?
Tokenization is the process of splitting text into smaller units called tokens according to a tokenizer's rules. These tokens are then represented as token IDs for model processing.

Q2. Is one token always one word?
No. A token can represent a whole word, part of a word, punctuation, or another text unit. Token boundaries depend on the tokenizer.

Q3. What is the difference between a tokenizer and an embedding?
A tokenizer converts text into tokens and token IDs. An embedding layer maps token IDs to numerical vectors that the model can process.

Intermediate level
Q4. Why do many LLMs use subword tokenization?
Subword tokenization helps balance vocabulary size and sequence length.
It can represent many unfamiliar words by combining reusable pieces rather than requiring every complete word to have its own vocabulary entry.

Q5. Why might two LLMs tokenize the same sentence differently?
They may use different vocabularies, tokenization algorithms, normalization rules, and special-token configurations.
Therefore, token counts from one model should not automatically be treated as exact counts for another.

Q6. Why might local token counting differ from OpenAI API usage?
Local counting generally covers the text passed to the tokenizer. 
The complete API request can include message formatting, tool definitions, images, files, or other structures that affect the actual input token count.

Three-year-experience scenario questions
Q7. Your RAG application exceeds the context limit. What would you do?
I would measure the token count of the complete prompt, including instructions, retrieved documents, conversation history, and the reserved output budget.
Then I would retrieve fewer or more relevant chunks, remove redundant context, summarize older history where appropriate, and enforce a token budget before making the model call.
I would evaluate retrieval quality and answer correctness after optimization.

Q8. Your token count increases significantly after changing models. Why?
The models may use different tokenizers and vocabularies. I would verify the tokenizer associated with each model, 
compare counts on a representative dataset, and measure the resulting effects on context capacity, latency, and API cost.

Q9. How would you choose between a local model and a hosted API?
I would compare quality, latency, throughput, hardware costs, operational complexity, privacy requirements, licensing, and scaling needs.
A local model may offer greater infrastructure control, while a hosted API can reduce the burden of managing inference infrastructure. 
I would benchmark both against the application's evaluation dataset before choosing.
